own, and he suspected that the wife of his rival, who happened to work as a X, was deliberately diverting business intended for him to her husband's establishment instead. Rather than complain to the authorities, he set out to remove his rival’s wife from the system entirely. By 1891 he had patented an electromechanical \"step-by-step\" device. By 1892 his device, the world’s first Y. was in service in La Porte, Indiana. Future refinements to the Y would eventually lead job losses for over 350,000 women who worked as Xs. ID X (10 points).", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-22T03:57:35Z", "username": "Nikhil Borra", "text": "Telephone operator", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-22T03:57:48Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "Answers: X=telephone switchboard operator", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-22T03:58:15Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "An upset undertaker developed an automated switch that eventually made switchboard operators redundant", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}]